README.OSK -- how this tree differs from TOP release 2's USR/SRC/moriaORIG/ is the tree exactly as TOP carried it: UMoria 4.87, Robert AlanKoeneke, with James E. Wilson's Unix port and Frank Kaefer's OS-9 port.patch.rej, a leftover of Kaefer's patching, is not carried here.The game's own code is unchanged.  Two things differ, both about buildingit and about how it finds the terminal:1. types.h is renamed mtypes.h, and every "types.h" include with it.  The   build puts this directory on the <...> search path, so the system   <types.h> that main.c and five other files ask for found this file   instead, before constants.h, and the program's own include then read it   a second time.2. tcentry.c is added.  It replaces read_entry() from TOP's ncurses.l, and   the recipe links it with LINKFIRST so that it, not the library's copy,   is the one used.  It is Reimer Mellin's routine with three changes, each   needed before the game would draw a screen with SYS/termcap:   - The library takes only an entry whose third character is '|' -- a     two-letter first name -- and matches TERM only after a '|'.     SYS/termcap opens "xterm-256color|xterm|vt100|...", so nothing matched.   - It decodes the termcap escape \n as this C's '\n', which is a carriage     return.  In termcap \n is a line feed, so "do=\n" never moved down a     line.  tcentry.c rewrites \n as ^J, in place.   - Where an entry has xn it hides am.  The library takes am to mean that     writing the last column moves the cursor to the next line; an xn     terminal holds that wrap and a cursor motion cancels it, so each row of     the town map landed at the right-hand edge.TOP's binary is not carried.  With SYS/termcap it cannot find the terminal,and given an entry it can read, it still runs its screens off the rightedge from the second line on.The data in USR/GAMES/MORIADIR is TOP's own: its score file, the newsscreen, and an hours file that allows play at any time.  The Moria_hours inthis tree closes the game from 8 to 5 on weekdays.